<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lauter (Rhine)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wieslauter</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lauter is a river that rises in the Palatinate Forest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and flows south-east past Wissembourg to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> near Lauterbourg. For much of its lower course it forms the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Germany. The Lauter Lines, a chain of French defensive earthworks, were built along it in the early 18th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lauter-rhine.jpg" alt="Lauter (Rhine)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Badener · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scheibenhard_Lauter_273.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
